<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenceslaus IV of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1361">1361</a>–<a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Wenceslaus, King of the Romans, Wenceslaus the Idle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenceslaus IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> from 1378 and King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from 1376 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> deposed him in 1400. In 1395 he sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> the title of Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, one of the grievances behind his deposition. His last years in Bohemia saw the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Wenceslaus IV of Bohemia" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Acoma · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaclav_of_Bohemia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
